<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="e3f2ca60">&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Simple Approach&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Use experienced judgment and/or previous history to determine the average cost per hour ($/hour) of the project, as
    well as the average number of hours of work that will be performed for each resource (hour/FTE). Once these averages
    have been estimated, the PM can use the following formulas:&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/PM-600-0023.png&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;412&quot; /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    This method is quick and easy, but not necessarily preferred because it lumps rates and hours together without having
    visibility into the rank and productivity of individuals, which can drive hours assigned and result in cost variances.
    For instance, a Senior Manager may be more efficient and costly than a Senior. Yet, this is not evident in the above
    example, which assesses everyone equally.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Refined approach&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The refined approach views team members individually in relation to the tasks assigned. A project cost is calculated by
    assigning individuals to tasks, documenting their rates, assessing hours for the activity (or task) and multiplying the
    hours by the individual’s rate. Once this process is completed for each individual assigned to the project, the totals
    can be added together for total project cost. This exercise can easily be accomplished using a project tool such as
    Microsoft Project. This will allow you to do a time phase estimate of resources and cost which can fed back into your
    engagement economics.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
